----------------------------------------------
This file is required by the autoconf utility.
Please do not remove this file.
----------------------------------------------

To run the test problems without building the source code,
see the last section: "Testing Without Building"

Both the code-generation and test-execution procedures require
GNU Make and will probably fail with other make programs.
Locate the name of the GNU make program on your system.
On Linux, GNU make is the standard make. On other Unix
workstations, GNU make is often gmake (so named to avoid
conflict with the standard make program) or is in another
directory such as /usr/local/bin/make. Ask your system
manager if you have trouble locating GNU Make.

The default target generates the system:

  make all                      Make codes and data files.

This target runs test problems, making codes and data as needed:

  make tests                    Run the standard tests, mesh tally
                                tests, and Htape tests.

The standard tests, mesh tally tests, and Htape tests can be run
separately by these targets:

  make single
  make meshtal
  make htape

respectively.

After the standard test set is run, the difm* and difo* files can
be listed and counted.  The difm* files compare to MCTAL files;
the difo* files compare to OUTP files.  If the test runs track
the standard code, then all of the difm* and difo* files will have
zero length.  The difm* and difo* files are listed and counted by

   make m     and     make o     respectively.

Similarly, after the test problems for the mesh tally have been run,
the difd* files comparing with MDAT files are listed and counted by

   make d

If the Htape test problems have been run, then the comparisons with
MCTAL files will be displayed by the "make m" target described above.
In addition, comparison with OUTT files is achieved by

   make t

Finally, the last part of the Htape test set includes cross-section
generation.  The difx* files testing this feature can be listed and
counted by

   make x

After any test set has been executed, it may be that not all of the
dif* files will have zero length.  The following invocation can be
used to examine the results in such a case:

  make examine                  VIEW each dif* file that has non-zero
                                length.  Make this target only after
                                you have run the set of test problems.
                                This also removes any dif* files that
                                have zero length.

-----------------------------------
Testing Without Building
-----------------------------------


You can test an MCNPX executable without first building it from the
source files by using the following procedure to run test cases
with the distributed executables which were created by LANL developers.

The mcnpx_2.4.0/README.w32 file includes additional information on 
software requirements and setting the correct path.

1. Copy files to the test directory. 
   Instructions on where to find files:
--------------------------------------------------
-- for beta test users 	
--------------------------------------------------
 Get files from WWW: mcnpx_2.4.0.tar.gz, DATA.tar.gz, SYS.tar.gz
   where SYS = AIX, DEC, HPUX, SGI, LINUX, SUN or Win32.ZIP.
   gunzip file.tar.gz and then tar xf file.tar (WinZip for PC Windows)

   get mcnpx, htape3x, xsex3      from SYS/bin
       bertin, phtlib                  SYS/data
       barpol.dat                      DATA
       input files:                    mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test/Test_sources/*
       test libraries:                 mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test/TestXS/*
       test templates                  mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test/Test/Test.SYS.../*
       runprob script                  mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test/Test/runprob


   For PC Windows get mcnpx.exe, htape3x.exe, xsex3.exe, and runprob.bat

--------------------------------------------------
-- for RSICC distribution users on Windows:
--------------------------------------------------

c:\
mkdir mcnpx_test
cd mcnpx_test
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\Data\LCS\barpol.dat
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\exe\windows\data\bertin 
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\exe\windows\data\phtlib 
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\exe\windows\bin\mcnpx.exe 
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\exe\windows\bin\htape3x.exe 
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\exe\windows\bin\xsex3.exe 
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\src\Test\TestXS\testdir 
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\src\Test\TestXS\testlib1
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\src\Test\Test_sources\*
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\src\Test\Test.intel.Windows.dvf.cc
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\exe\runprob.bat
copy ..\MCNPX_2.4.0\bin\x11.dll

The x11.dll file is required for interactive graphics available 
in MCNPX. It is included in the self-extracting Win32.exe file 
distributed in mcnpx_2.4.0.  The make procedure expands the 
file. Alternately, one can expand it by typing the file name.


--------------------------------------------------
-- for RSICC distribution users on Unix or Linux:
--------------------------------------------------
LANL test results are in the mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test 
subdirectory.  The following example copies the Linux
results. Select the appropriate results for your system.
X11 software is required and is generally available
with Unix and Linux systems.

mkdir mcnpx_test
cd mcnpx_test
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/Data/LCS/barpol.dat .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test/Test_sources/* .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test/Test.intel.linux.pgf90.pgcc/* .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test/TestXS/* .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/src/Test/runprob .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/exe/linux/data/bertin .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/exe/linux/data/phtlib .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/exe/linux/bin/mcnpx .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/exe/linux/bin/htape3x .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/exe/linux/bin/xsex3 .
cp ../mcnpx_2.4.0/exe/runprob .


--------------------------------------------------
--------------------------------------------------

2. Execute the test problems:

   runprob

3. You have successfully run the test problems if you have the following
   *m files created:

   htpNNm   where NN = 01 - 06
   inpNNm   where NN = 01 - 47 
   mesNNm   where NN = 01 - 16

   and if all diff files are length 0 (unix) or length 69 (Windows).

